ider us  only agricultural implements  here in this park today we make a solemn  promise to enjoy a rightful part of the  riches of this rich land and to throw  off the yoke of being considered as  agricultural implements  another key to understanding  the cultural politics of chavez's world  was the union's effort to draw attention  to rampant pesticide poisoning in rural  communities and particularly to the  cancer clusters in towns like mcfarland  california  the union coded these as threats not  just to the farm workers who live there  but ultimately to everyone in the united  states to all consumers of california  produce  if the bodies of farm workers were  exposed to parathyan to ddt  or to other quote economic poisons as  chavez called them  then those chemicals just as surely will  find their way into middle class homes  on the east coast  this critical argument  connected the fates of workers bodies  with those of far away consumers this  emphasis which ultimately drew  from chavez's catholic thinking about  the body of christ which links all  people in some mystical fashion  here not just through the eucharist  through the food but through the sharing  of california produce what comes to our  table remained the critical subtext of  hundreds of union mailings of flyers and  speeches during the late 20th century  and came to inspire a great deal of art  as well  all of this  all of these campaigns this religious  rhetoric proved enormously effective for  the union at least for a time  the language of conscience and of  justice  for forced george meaney and other labor  leaders to invest millions of dollars  into farmworker unionization despite  their doubts about it  and about chavez  the catholic bishops became strong  supporters  east coast rabbis shut down great  purchases in new york city in boston and  elsewhere  hundreds of synagogues and temples  across the nation  refused to use non-union grapes for a  sacramental wine  and the massachusetts board of rabbis  published a formal legal opinion based  on talmudic prince precedence that  supported the boycott of grapes  deeply immersed one leader of the  american jewish community committee  called all this quote a model of  sophisticated effective cooperation  between catholics protestants and jews  joining hands in the common work of  social justice  chavez joked that he more than anyone  else was responsible to get for getting  many of these people to talk with one  another for the first time across  religious lines  now of course the site of roman callers  of nuns of rabbis and ministers on union  picket lines drove growers to  distraction  one anti-ufw group declared quote  ministers of the gospel are the greatest  dupes for the socialistic and  communistic manipulators in california  union opponents  hired uh skilled and expensive public  relations experts in new york city to  craft a portrait of a more devious and  more militant and entirely unspiritual  cesar chavez  these portraits called chavez a false  prophet and questioned his unionist  credentials  quote his appeal is primarily racial  one grower told the new york times  they accused him of violating their  constitutional rights and they charged  chavez with harassing innocent  unsuspecting housewives coast to coast  who simply wanted to feed their families  conservative spokespeople for some of  the growers  some of these spokespeople mexican  americans went on national speaking  tours to denounce what they called quote  the chavez community forces  and the union's quote black bird of  death  now those arguments those counter  arguments about religiosity never really  dislodged chavez from his moral perch  during his life  reviewing the african-american freedom  struggle of the post-world war ii period  historian david chappelle  has argued that quote white supremacists  in the south  failed to get their churches to give  their cause active support that was  their achilles heel  and i think in much the same way  growers and teamsters found it all but  impossible to sway many religious  leaders into anti-chavez positions  few prominent national religious  organizations refused to support chavez  during his lifetime  the decision of the southern california  arizona united methodist church was  typical  members of that annual conference  declared their support for the union  based on chavez's earnest christian  stalwart character  chavez's connections with religiosity  did prompt real controversy on the left  traditional unionists often hated these  signs of religiosity and all the god  talk in the movement  some young activists believed  religiosity had no place in the  organization at all  and sometimes travis got himself in  trouble for speaking about things he  didn't understand  following the start of the yom kippur  war  many criticized chavez's controversial  statement on israel that urged quote all  our brothers and sisters in the  farmworker movement and all people of  goodwill throughout the world to stand  with israel in its struggle to  live and to be free  still despite this controversy  from 1965 into the 1990s the ufw was  able to occupy high moral ground on  issues related to labor relations  immigration and more  making the organization what the afl-cio  would later call quote the conscience of  the labor movement  when chavez was imprisoned for two weeks  for calling a nationwide lettuce boycott  workers created altars and held 24-hour  vigils outside the monterey county jail  catholic school kids wrote chava's  letters of support at the prompting of  the nuns  ethel kennedy attended mass outside the  jail coretta scott king bestowed her  blessing on chavez quote  two thousand years ago in bethlehem a  man spoke to the lowly about poverty and  human dignity jesus of nazareth was  crucified but he is here with us in  salinas today  because the lowly need him again  during another struggle near near  bakersfield an elderly dorothy day was  among 60 religious leaders jailed for  participating in ufw picket lines she  announced that quote when you see  churchmen in jail you feel it has become  a real christianity  and rome's approval certainly helped  in chavez's private papal audience  in september of 1974  pope paul vi called him quote a loyal  son of the catholic church and a  distinguished leader and representative  of the mexican-american community  and the pontiff indicated quote we're  all indeed grateful to mr chavez for the  lesson which he brings to our attention  so i hope by now that i've convinced you  that chavez's movement  this foundational piece of modern latino  politics was religious to the core and  may still be  and that you might be able to see why it  provided a language of dignity of  liberation of solidarity  even as it provided a framework for  institutional and individual support by  outsiders  chavez's insistence  that god is on the side of the poor  that what seems impossible to achieve  will actually be possible  that legal provisions alone don't  capture the limit of what's right and  just  and that we're intimately connected with  those who are unrecognized in our  society  these are powerful starting points  i think you'll agree for social  movements  and they remain so today  if you listen closely  you can hear echoes of these arguments i  think in the many boycott campaigns  ongoing in the 21st century since the  early 2000s  you can certainly identify these strains  in the immigration rights movement which  produced in 2006 the largest public  demonstrations in u.s history and which  prompted 20 new haveners just this week  including yale students to submit to  arrest in washington dc  and you can hear it in the democratic  party's efforts to connect to capture  the latino vote  most famously in barack obama's adoption  of the united farm workers phrase  si se  as yes we can  onwards  this phrase suggests the people will be  heard  nothing is impossible  the future is bright  have courage  all of which brings us back  in what may seem a paradoxical way  to the death of chavez in 1993.  his gravesite  which is also the ufw's working center  in keene california not far from  bakersfield  was declared  a national monument by president obama  last fall  it was a nod  to the unrecognized importance of latino  history as united states history  it was an acknowledgment that the union  and its supporters had played an  important role in electing obama twice  but it was also an affirmation of  chavez's perceived importance today in  the 21st century as something like a  beacon an inspiration a model or a guide  standing not far from chavez's grave  exactly almost exactly a year ago  president obama  declared  that quote  the truth is we would not be here if it  weren't for caesar  and that the message that chesiva caesar  conveys for the 21st century is in many  respects a religious one  quote  every time somebody's son or daughter  comes and learns about the history of  this movement  i want them to know that our journey is  never hopeless our work is never done  i want them to learn about a small man  guided by enormous faith  in a righteous cause a loving god  the dignity of every human being  i want them to remember that true  courage is revealed when the night is  darkest and the resistance is strongest  and we somehow find it within ourselves  to stand up for what we believe in  president obama  in declaring this a national landmark  concluded that quote  our world is a better place because  caesar chavez decided to change it let's  honor his memory but most importantly  let's live up to his example  and in the phrase and in the language  that perhaps best suggests chavez's  importance for the 20th 21st century  obama concluded his remarks with  si se puede  thank you

